== B. two laws for one quantity: what regime R allows
   (i) knowing f_A and f_B: e_B decoded from d(t) = f_A - f_B on 400000 of 400000 data; max |q_dec - q| = 4.55e-13
       so a theory machine stating both laws (and the decoding) proves every cell: rate 0 < both laws' rates; SAI's own rate here is 0, not min(r_A, r_B)
   (ii) A's errors i.i.d. U[-1,1] (random); B's errors e_A - d(t), d(t) in {-M,0,M} w.p. {.1,.8,.1}:
       Delta   0.1: calibrated A 4.3582 +- 0.0004 | calibrated B 5.2758 +- 0.0019 | B - A = +0.9176 (H(d) = 0.9219)
       Delta   1.0: calibrated A 1.3616 +- 0.0010 | calibrated B 2.2793 +- 0.0021 | B - A = +0.9177 (H(d) = 0.9219)
       Delta   1.5: calibrated A 0.9565 +- 0.0011 | calibrated B 1.8764 +- 0.0022 | B - A = +0.9198 (H(d) = 0.9219)
       Delta   2.0: calibrated A 0.7223 +- 0.0011 | calibrated B 1.6382 +- 0.0022 | B - A = +0.9159 (H(d) = 0.9219)
       Delta  10.0: calibrated A 0.1445 +- 0.0007 | calibrated B 1.0631 +- 0.0020 | B - A = +0.9187 (H(d) = 0.9219)
       B never beats A: with A's errors random, r_A is Prop. 9.2(c)'s lower bound for EVERY machine; a ranking
       of two laws' calibrated machines that differs from 'A first' needs errors that are not random for either.

